{"name":"Barley Groats","hex":"#fbf2db","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cereal of the species Hordeum vulgare or its grains, often used as food or to make beer and other malted drinks.","The seed of Job's tears (Coix lacryma-jobi)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barley"},{"term":"groats","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of groat"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/groats"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barley","blocks":[{"text":"Barley, a member of the grass family, is a major cereal grain grown in temperate climates globally. One of the first cultivated grains, it was domesticated in the Fertile Crescent around 9000 BC, giving it nonshattering spikelets and making it much easier to harvest. Its use then spread throughout Eurasia by 2000 BC. Barley prefers relatively low temperatures and well-drained soil to grow. It is relatively tolerant of drought and soil salinity, but is less winter-hardy than wheat or rye. Wild barley may refer to H. vulgare ssp. spontaneum, but may also refer to other species in the genus Hordeum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg/330px-Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barley"},{"title":"Groat (grain)","blocks":[{"text":"Groats are the hulled kernels of various cereal grains, such as oats, wheat, rye, and barley. Groats are whole grains that include the cereal germ and fiber-rich bran portion of the grain, as well as the endosperm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Pigeons_food_cereals_mix.jpg/330px-Pigeons_food_cereals_mix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groat_(grain)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Barley Barn at Cressing, Essex, built around 1220; its name means \"barley barley-store\".[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarley_Barn_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Botanical illustration of leafy stem with roots, flowers, and 2- and 6-row ears","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg/500px-Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg/1280px-Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https:/